# 📊 Employee Performance Analysis

> A complete statistical analysis of employee performance data using Python — covering descriptive statistics, probability, distributions, and linear algebra.

---

## 📁 Project Structure

```
📦 Employee Performance Analysis
 ┣ 📓 Employee_Performance.ipynb   ← Main notebook
 ┗ 📂 Data
    ┗ 📄 employee_performance.csv  ← Dataset
```

---

## 🧠 Topics Covered

### 📖 Part A — Theory

| # | Topic | Description |
|---|-------|-------------|
| 1 | 📐 Mean, Median & Mode | Measures of central tendency |
| 2 | 📏 Range & Variance | Spread of data |
| 3 | 🔔 Normal vs Poisson Distribution | Continuous vs discrete distributions |
| 4 | 〰️ Skewness | Asymmetry of distributions |
| 5 | 🎲 Conditional Probability | P(A\|B) with employee promotion example |
| 6 | 🔗 Independent vs Mutually Exclusive | Event relationships |
| 7 | 🧮 Bayes' Theorem | Updating probabilities with evidence |
| 8 | 🔻 PCA | Dimensionality reduction |

---

### 💻 Part B — Practical Steps

#### 🔢 Step 1 · Central Tendency & Dispersion
- ✅ Mean, Median, and Mode of **Salary**
- ✅ Variance and Standard Deviation of **Projects_Completed**

#### 🎲 Step 2 · Probability & Events
- ✅ Probability of employee promotion
- ✅ Contingency table — `Promotion_Status` × `Department`
- ✅ Conditional Probability → `P(Promotion | Performance_Score > 80)`

#### 📈 Step 3 · Distributions & Visualization
- ✅ Histogram of **Performance_Score** with Gaussian (KDE) curve
- ✅ Skewness & Kurtosis of **Salary** (manual + SciPy)
- ✅ Q-Q Plot of **Projects_Completed**

#### 📐 Step 4 · Linear Algebra Application
- ✅ Employee work vectors: `[Projects_Completed, Working_Hours]`
- ✅ Dot product between employee vectors (manual + NumPy)
- ✅ L1 & L2 Norms of work vectors
- ✅ Angle between two employee vectors (with quiver plot)

---
## 🏁 Conclusion

This project demonstrates how core mathematical and statistical concepts apply to real-world employee data:

- 📐 **Descriptive statistics** revealed the central tendency and spread of salary and project data.
- 🎲 **Probability analysis** showed the likelihood of promotion and how performance score influences it.
- 📈 **Distribution plots** (Histogram, Q-Q Plot) helped visualize data shape and normality.
- 🔢 **Linear algebra** techniques like dot products, norms, and vector angles gave a geometric view of employee work patterns.

Overall, this analysis builds a strong foundation for understanding data science and machine learning concepts through a practical HR dataset.
